# Line endings, decided once so they cannot be decided again by accident.
#
# ARGOS hashes file bytes and checks the hash: every recorded payload under
# `tests/fixtures/` and `docs/research/fixtures/` carries a `raw_sha256`, and
# `tests/test_fixtures.py` fails when one stops matching. Git's default
# `core.autocrlf=true` on a Windows checkout rewrites LF to CRLF on the way to
# the working tree, which changes those bytes and therefore that hash.
#
# That is not a cosmetic preference. Core invariant 7 says raw data is immutable
# and `.claude/rules/data-integrity.md` says raw source payloads are linked by
# hash; a checkout that silently rewrites them breaks both before a single test
# runs. Measured on a fresh clone with the Git-for-Windows default: all four
# recorded fixtures failed their own integrity check — `book_yes.raw.json`
# arrived as 7,532 bytes hashing 8f95c861... against the 7,174 bytes hashing
# 510f03e4... its sidecar records.

# Source, documentation and configuration are normalized to LF in the
# repository *and* checked out as LF on every platform. `eol=lf` rather than
# leaving the working tree to the platform, because the working tree is what
# formatters, type checkers and editors rewrite: a Windows checkout with CRLF
# working files produces CRLF edits, and this repository has already lost a
# commit's worth of diff to exactly that.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Recorded evidence is byte-exact and is never translated in either direction.
# Scoped to the two fixture trees rather than applied repository-wide, so that
# normalization still governs everything it should — but scoped by *directory*
# rather than by extension, because the guarantee has to hold for a payload
# recorded next month by someone who did not read this file.
tests/fixtures/** -text
docs/research/fixtures/** -text
